// Overcast: a cold, flat, heavily clouded day.
//
// Same format as `config/lighting.ron` — a scenario names one of these in
// `scenarios.ron` and gets it instead of the shipped default.
//
// # Why weather and not a time of day
//
// A first sunset attempt exposed a reason worth writing down: **the map camera looks
// down, so most of the visible dome is below the true horizon.** `zenith_color` is
// barely on screen and `sky_color` is most of the surround. Giving that entire band a
// warm orange produced green terrain on a flat terracotta field, not evening. The
// cyclic clear profile now solves that with an azimuth-local mirrored glow, while this
// file intentionally remains a flat static weather profile.
//
// So a second sky has to stay sky-*coloured*. Weather changes the light without asking
// the background to be a colour skies are not, at the one angle you can see.
//
// The tell that the SUN is being re-applied and not just the dome: shadows point a
// different way and are much fainter. If the sky changes and the shadows do not, the
// dome is being repainted while the directional light is not.
(
    // Weaker and slightly cool. Overcast light is diffuse; the sun is behind cloud
    // rather than absent, so it still casts, just faintly.
    sun_illuminance: 5500.0,
    sun_color: (0.94, 0.96, 1.0),

    // **These three numbers are not "height, compass, roll".** They are an XYZ Euler
    // triple, they wrap past 2pi, and the vertical component of the result depends on
    // both of the first two together. The first version of this file changed both and
    // put the sun **20 degrees below the horizon** — which lights nothing, renders a
    // black map, and reports no error at all.
    //
    // So the first number is left exactly as the shipped default and only the second is
    // swung. That puts the sun about 25 degrees up instead of 61, so shadows fall long
    // and in a different direction. `every_shipped_sun_is_above_the_horizon` checks
    // this rather than trusting it.
    sun_rotation: (11.4, 1.1, 0.0),

    // Much more fill, very slightly blue. This is what actually sells overcast: under
    // cloud almost all the light is sky-scattered, so shadows are shallow rather than
    // sharp. It is also why the terrain stays readable on a weaker sun.
    ambient_brightness: 190.0,
    ambient_color: (0.92, 0.95, 1.0),

    // Still off. See CONTENT.md before turning it up.
    sky_light_intensity: 0.0,
    ground_color: (0.28, 0.28, 0.26),

    // Desaturated and pale — grey-blue rather than blue. Kept *lighter* than the
    // shipped sky, because a dark overcast sky and near-white cloud is a stormfront,
    // which is a different thing and much louder.
    sky_color: (0.74, 0.79, 0.83),
    zenith_color: (0.52, 0.60, 0.68),

    // Heavy cover, low contrast against the sky. Larger cells than the default so the
    // cloud reads as a single sheet with breaks in it rather than as separate puffs.
    cloud_color: (0.98, 0.98, 1.0),
    cloud_coverage: 0.46,
    hex_cloud_scale: 13.0,
    cloud_softness: 0.22,
    cloud_roundness: 0.7,
    cloud_noise: 0.45,

    // Haze stays off, as it does everywhere. The fields are here so the file is a
    // complete `LightingSettings` and can be experimented with in place.
    fog_color: (0.78, 0.81, 0.84),
    fog_sun_color: (0.90, 0.92, 0.95),
    fog_density: 0.0,
)
